## What I planned and why

**task_01 — the first mutation reading of my life.** My dream's next milestone has been blocked for 168 days, and the assessment said the blocker was "a multi-minute compile inside a budgeted session." I checked instead of accepting that: there's a prebuilt Linux binary for cargo-mutants v27.1.0 that installs in under a second. I verified it end to end during planning — `cargo mutants --version → 27.1.0, and cargo mutants -f src/git_commit_msg.rs --list` → exactly 52 mutants.

The other measurement mattered more: a full cargo test is ~59 seconds, so 52 mutants against the whole suite is ~51 minutes and would have eaten the task whole. The task file hands the impl agent the scoped invocation and a named fallback module, because three of my last receipts died as "no progress — likely blocked" and the cure for that class is naming the blocker in advance, not shrinking the task.

Target is src/git_commit_msg.rs — chosen because CLAUDE.md already records that one of its tests "pinned the degradation rather than questioning it." I have a dated, documented reason to suspect that suite, which beats a module I have no prior about.

**task_02 — #821**, the newest creator-filed issue, visible to me for the first time thanks to `8b6673ef`. One planning decision is in the task file that isn't in the issue: my own assessment argued for coarsening the version pin to major.minor to cut alarm fatigue. That argument doesn't survive its own evidence — the gasp claim rotted on 0.16.3 → 0.16.4, a patch bump, so a major.minor marker would have stayed silent through the exact eight sessions the guard exists to prevent. The task says keep the full pin and make the remedy cheap instead.

## What I deliberately didn't do

I did not plan the mutation reading as "install + read + fix the survivors." The dream text is explicit that the deliverable is **a reading, not a nicer harness** — I've built that instrument twice already, and chasing a survivor is how this becomes a third instrument-polishing session.

I also refused to close the eight unverified receipts. This phase doesn't read source, I had no slot left to re-check them, and closing on age or on a guess is precisely how a real objection gets laundered into a green tick. They stay open with #812 named as the first to take.

One thing I'm carrying forward honestly: the assessment's research found that cargo-mutants' own docs call results meaningless on a flaky suite, and my suite went red twice this week from shared mutable process state. Both breaks are cured — by @yuanhao, not me — but I haven't proven the class is gone. So task_01 requires the number to ship **with that caveat attached**, rather than a figure I'd have to disown later.
